2026-06-30 15:35:10,448 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-643083f0-eb89-43ab-a8bb-4e601550690e', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample1.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample10.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample11.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample12.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample2.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample3.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample4.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample5.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample6.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample7.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample8.raw\nGMCAVK1_12_20220216_5out15_3hgrad_sample9.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S1.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S2.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S3.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S4.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S5.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S6.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S7.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S8.raw\nkaveh_20210120_labelfree_3hLG_5of10uL_S9.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nUV-inducible crosslinking IP-MS to elucidate the role of nuclear Hsp104 during quiescence in Saccharomyces cerevisiae\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn this study, we utilize high-content microscopy screening and quantitative proteomics to establish a function of nuclear Hsp104 during aging. We find that the cytosolic-nuclear partitioning of Hsp104 is critical to maintain nuclear proteostasis in quiescent cells. Whereas high global translation rates in rapidly growing cells constrain Hsp104 to the cytosol, a decrease in protein biosynthesis re-directs the disaggregase to the nucleus dependent on a specific C-terminal motif. Nuclear Hsp104 interacts with latent eIF2 translation initiation subcomplexes and suppresses protein aggregation. This protects the dormant translation machinery from age-induced damage, enabling the rapid resumption of protein synthesis upon re-entry into the cell cycle. In this project two separate IP-MS experiments are included, KGSBVK (IP-MS, 9 samples) and GMCAVK (UV-inducible crosslinking IP-MS, 12 samples).\n\n[METHODS]\nSample preparation for mass spectrometry Both: The bead-bound proteins were reduced in 1 mM DTT at room temperature for 30 minutes. Next, samples were alkylated by incubation in the dark at room temperature in 5 mM Chloroacetamide for 20 minutes. After incubation the remaining Chloroacetamide was quenched by the addition of 5 mM DTT. Digestion was carried out by the addition of 0.5-2 µg Trypsin (sequencing grade modified, Pierce) and over-night incubation at 37°C. After bead-digestion, peptide clean-up was performed by a modified sp3 protocol (Moggridge et al., 2018). Briefly, the peptides were resuspended in 30 µl of 50mM HEPES buffer solution and 10 µl Sera‐Mag SP3 bead mix was added to the sample. Next, 100% acetonitrile was added to achieve a final concentration of >95%. Samples were pipette-mixed and incubated for 18-20 minutes at room temperature and then placed on a magnetic rack. The supernatant was aspirated and discarded, and the beads were washed in 200 µl of acetonitrile. Samples were removed from the magnetic rack and beads were reconstituted in 200 µl of MQ water. Then the beads were placed on a magnetic rack again and the supernatant was recovered and transferred to an MS-vial and dried in a vacuum centrifuge. The dried peptides were then resuspended in 15 µl of a 3% ACN and 0.1% formic acid solution. Mass spectrometry based quantitative proteomics LC-ESI-MS/MS Q-Exactive Online LC-MS was performed using a Dionex Ultimate™ 3000 RSLCnano System coupled to a Q-Exactive mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). 5 µL was injected from each sample. Samples were trapped on a C18 guard desalting column (Acclaim PepMap 100, 75um x 2 cm, nanoViper, C18, 5 µm, 100 Å), and separated on a 50 cm long C18 column (Easy spray PepMap RSLC, C18, 2 µm, 100Å, 75 µmx50cm). The nano capillary solvent A was 95% water, 5%DMSO, 0.1% formic acid; and solvent B was 5% water, 5% DMSO, 95% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid. At a constant flow of 0.25 μl min−1, the curved gradient went from 6%B up to 43%B in 180 min, followed by a steep increase to 100%B in 5 min. FTMS master scans with 60,000 resolution (and mass range 300-1500 m/z) were followed by data-dependent MS/MS (30 000 resolution) on the top 5 ions using higher energy collision dissociation (HCD) at 30% normalized collision energy. Precursors were isolated with a 2m/z window. Automatic gain control (AGC) targets were 1e6 for MS1 and 1e5 for MS2. Maximum injection times were 100ms for MS1 and MS2. The entire duty cycle lasted ~2.5s. Dynamic exclusion was used with 60s duration. Precursors with unassigned charge state or charge state 1 were excluded. An underfill ratio of 1% was used.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
